    {
      "id": "EZS-02",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "anchor": "inputs-exceptional-zero-repulsion",
      "title": "Quantitative survival of the exceptional factor in the weighted zero sum",
      "statement": "If Y=log t≥K log(m) log(log(m)+2), the finite weighted zero sum after removal of the exceptional occurrence is at most Cη exp(−cY/log(m)), with η=min(1,(1−β)log(m)) or η=1 in its absence. The large-modulus branch of the weighted progression estimate retains this η.",
      "hypotheses": "The explicit formula lists satisfy the preceding fixed-modulus count. Use the zero-free region with at most one simple real exceptional zero, the Deuring–Heilbronn inequality with a logarithmic power loss and adequately large fixed K. The transfer of the remaining explicit-formula error also uses the large-modulus condition log(m)>c_A√Y and the lower bound η≥c′/m.",
